Clean patch: guard the Linux-specific includes and the PinToBigCores body with `#ifdef __linux__` (or `#if defined(__linux__)`). On Windows, PinToBigCores becomes a no-op, and `getpid()` won't be called.

Let me patch:
- Lines 6-7: `#include <sched.h>` and `#include <unistd.h>` — guard with `#ifdef __linux__`. (`<unistd.h>` exists in mingw but doesn't have sched stuff; safe to include but let me guard sched.h only. Actually unistd.h in mingw provides getpid? mingw's unistd.h may or may not have getpid. Let me guard both includes for cleanliness, and use a no-op PinToBigCores on non-linux.)

Patch:
```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#ifdef __linux__
#include <sched.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#endif

namespace {

int64_t GetTimestamp() {
  return std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now().time_since_epoch().count();
}

void PinToBigCores() {
#ifdef __linux__
  cpu_set_t set;
  CPU_ZERO(&set);
  // cpu12-13: 2.75 GHz big cores
  CPU_SET(12, &set);
  CPU_SET(13, &set);
  if (sched_setaffinity(getpid(), sizeof(set), &set) != 0) {
    std::cerr << "Warning: failed to pin CPU benchmark to big cores" << std::endl;
  }
#endif
}

}  // namespace
```

This is minimal and clean — on Windows it's a no-op (no core pinning, which is fine for a benchmark reference; we mainly care about the vulkan backend). On Linux behavior is unchanged.

